{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"operatic-even-ballpython","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## The defect\n\n`.github/workflows/_build-and-test.yml`, the `sandbox-enforcement` job, discards ALL output from\nits two `apt-get` calls:\n\n```\n339:  docker run --rm --privileged ubuntu:24.04 bash -c '\n340:    set -e\n341:    apt-get update -qq >/dev/null 2>&1\n342:    apt-get install -y -qq bubblewrap >/dev/null 2>&1\n343:    bwrap --version\n```\n\nWith `set -e`, a failure in either line aborts the container and the step fails with apt's exit\ncode and **nothing whatsoever in the log** to say what went wrong. `2>&1` sends stderr to\n`/dev/null` too, so even the error message is gone.\n\n## Observed, 2026-09-05\n\nChange 2652's `sandbox enforcement (Linux bwrap, gating)` cell — a GATING cell — failed with\n`exit code 100`. 100 is apt-get's generic error. The log contains no apt output at all, so the\nfailure could not be diagnosed from it. It was ultimately triaged as a transient package-manager\nfault only by inference: the step reads nothing from the patchset (stock container, Ubuntu\narchive), the change touched only `tests/`, `docs/` and a YAML comment, and the same job had been\n`success` on run `33938327905` eight minutes earlier. That is circumstantial reasoning standing in\nfor evidence the step deliberately threw away.\n\n## Why this one is worth fixing rather than tolerating\n\nRead the job's own comment at `:301-305`. It explains that this job exists precisely BECAUSE the\nprevious arrangement produced an unreadable signal:\n\n> \"those runners deny unprivileged CLONE_NEWUSER, so `probe()` returns None, every enforcement\n> test SKIPS, and the leg goes green having proven nothing — the signal for a broken sandbox is\n> indistinguishable from the signal for a healthy one.\"\n\nThe job written to remove an indistinguishable signal contains its own indistinguishable signal.\nWhen it fails, \"the Ubuntu archive was briefly unavailable\" and \"bubblewrap has been removed from\nthe archive\" and \"the container image changed under us\" all look identical: `exit code 100`.\n\nIt is also a GATING cell, so an unreadable failure blocks every change on that patchset while\ngiving no one anything to act on.\n\n## The fix is small and the tradeoff is real\n\n`-qq` already suppresses routine chatter; the `>/dev/null 2>&1` on top of it is what removes the\ndiagnostics. The obvious change is to drop the redirection (keeping `-qq`), or to keep stdout\nquiet while letting stderr through (`>/dev/null` without `2>&1`), so a failure explains itself\nwhile a success stays terse. The reason someone wrote it this way — apt is noisy and this is a hot\ngating path — is legitimate, so the fix should preserve quiet-on-success rather than simply\ndeleting the redirect and accepting the noise.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A failing `apt-get` in this job leaves its error message in the CI log. Proven by\n      deliberately breaking the install (e.g. an unresolvable package name) and reading the\n      captured output, not by asserting the redirect was removed.\n- [ ] A SUCCESSFUL run stays as quiet as it is today — no regression into a wall of apt output on\n      the hot gating path. Proven by comparing log length on a green run before and after.\n- [ ] The same audit is applied to the rest of this job and its siblings: any other\n      `>/dev/null 2>&1` on a command whose failure would abort a gating step is either justified\n      in a comment or fixed. Enumerate what you found and its disposition — do not fix only the\n      two lines named here.\n- [ ] The job's existing comment at `:301-305` is extended (or a new one added) noting that its\n      own diagnostics must stay readable, so the next person tightening this path does not\n      reintroduce the silence for the same good reason.","id":"7fd3-078a-941e-400f","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"The gating bwrap sandbox job discards all apt output, so an apt failure aborts it with exit 100 and no explanation -- inside a job whose own comment exists because an unreadable signal was the previous bug"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1788577422669198001,"uuid":"0cdfab92-dc01-4102-871d-d33e89ee54eb"}